INT. FARMHOUSE CELLAR - NIGHT

Damp stone. Shelves of jarred peaches. A single bare bulb swings from a cord.

Four people stand in the middle of the cellar. FAITH BROOKS (30s, flannel, a lantern in one hand). Her husband WALT (30s, ball cap, a shovel). Her little sister JUNIPER "JUNE" (16, braids, a stuffed rabbit). And an older man they found at the door, named only as the DRIFTER (60s, long coat, calm).

FAITH
Walt, shut the hatch.

WALT
It's shut.

JUNE
Faith, I don't like it down here.

FAITH
Hush, June.

The Drifter inspects a jar. Holds it to the bulb. Something inside moves.

DRIFTER
Nice peaches.

WALT
They're fine.

DRIFTER
They're alive.

Faith's lantern wavers. June clutches the rabbit.

FAITH
Who are you?

DRIFTER
(smiling)
Call me Ephraim.

WALT
Ephraim, put that down.

EPHRAIM
Walter, you have no idea what you're keeping.

Ephraim sets the jar back. The bulb flickers. Behind the shelves, something scrapes the wall, patient and slow.
